{:edb/db-before-t 1848 :edb/db-after-t 1849 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535161 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:21:28.859Z" 13194139535161 true] [17592186133552 1100 "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/assets/1e710ca7b3b7bea626de36181a30869b0f114a4497843c6b6ea8b8966a91d208.png) under this inner product." 13194139535161 true] [17592186133552 1100 "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." 13194139535161 false] [17592186133690 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334592 13194139535161 true] [17592186133690 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334641 13194139535161 true] [17592186133691 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334620 13194139535161 true] [17592186133691 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334648 13194139535161 true] [17592186133692 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334599 13194139535161 true] [17592186133692 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334613 13194139535161 true] [17592186133737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334606 13194139535161 true] [17592186133737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334627 13194139535161 true] [17592186133737 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334634 13194139535161 true] [17592186134326 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535161 true] [17592186134326 1100 "Let $V = R_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3)$.\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t) = 1 - 2t$." 13194139535161 false] [17592186134326 1100 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2)+x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=1-2t.$" 13194139535161 true] [17592186134326 1145 "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=1-2t,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=-1,\\,p(2)=-3,\\,p(3)=-5.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+9+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535161 true] [17592186134328 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535161 true] [17592186134328 1100 "Let $V = C[π, 2π]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[π, 2π]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = ∫_{π}^{2π}x(t)y(t)dt$.\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t) = \\cos t$." 13194139535161 false] [17592186134328 1100 "Let $V=C[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[\\pi ,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" 13194139535161 true] [17592186134328 1145 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{t}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{4}){|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi }{4})-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =(\\pi +0)-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡t=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535161 true] [17592186334592 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334593 13194139535161 true] [17592186334592 1089 "q-141169" 13194139535161 true] [17592186334592 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535161 true] [17592186334592 1100 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=2-3t+{t}^{2}.$" 13194139535161 true] [17592186334592 1113 #uuid "dea39803-23bf-5454-93d0-89ff1b7931a7" 13194139535161 true] [17592186334592 1145 "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2-3t+{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=0,\\,p(0)=2,\\,p(-1)=6.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(0)p(0)+p(-1)p(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(0)]^{2}+[p(-1)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535161 true] [17592186334593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334594 13194139535161 true] [17592186334593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334595 13194139535161 true] [17592186334593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334596 13194139535161 true] [17592186334593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334597 13194139535161 true] [17592186334593 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334598 13194139535161 true] [17592186334593 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535161 true] [17592186334593 1041 "selection" 13194139535161 true] [17592186334593 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334596 13194139535161 true] [17592186334593 11